WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.680
You will hear two Theatre Studies students, Maya and Finn, discussing their project on

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:15.880
theatre programmes. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 26.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 26.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:17.000
So, Finn, I've done as much as I can for our project on theatre programmes. How's your research coming along?

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:25.000
OK, Maya. I didn't know theatre programmes are called playbills in the USA till I started looking into the topic.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Even though I struggled to find many useful websites, I'm glad we picked this subject.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.000
No one else on the course is doing the same as us, although it is one of the research areas of the module convener.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.000
That might actually put some people off.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
I suppose so. Anyway, I hadn't realised there are actually companies specialising in creating theatre programmes.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Yes, they're quite common nowadays. Contrary to what many people think, theatres don't hire people to do the programmes.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
In fact, companies buy the rights to publish programmes on the theatre's behalf,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
and then make their money selling advertising space within the programme booklet.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
It must be easier for theatres to do it that way.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Yes.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
I remember reading something about programmes in early British theatre.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
It said that the cast was always very important.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Yeah. Audiences were very familiar with leading actors, and big names would draw huge crowds.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.000
But I hadn't realised that if the programme named a famous actor, that's who the public expected to perform.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.000
And if that didn't happen, people accused the theatre of breaking their agreement with the audience.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
They would demand refunds, and if they didn't get them, there were riots.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Outrageous! That'd never happen now.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:51.000
No, people are too polite, even when they're disappointed if the star of the show misses a performance.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
We should definitely include that information about early audiences in our project.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.000
I also think it's important to mention that lots of ordinary people at that time were illiterate,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.000
so theatre programmes were of limited value in advertising plays.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.000
When a company of actors arrived in a town, they'd parade around the streets in their costumes,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
beating drums and announcing their upcoming performances.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Interesting. I couldn't imagine that happening now either.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
There's also an interesting comparison to make between 18th and 19th century programmes.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Wasn't it in the 19th century that theatre programmes started to resemble programmes today?

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Yes, and unlike programmes from the 18th century, they always used colour.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.000
And there was a greater variety of designs.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.000
But personally, I think 18th century programmes were superior

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
because they told the theatre-goers so many things, including about the actors.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.000
And about the writer, the plot, and sometimes the history of the play.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.000
That's right. What should we say about theatre programmes in the 20th century?

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.000
I reckon the most important thing is the dramatic change they underwent during World War II.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.000
When the government imposed restrictions on the use of paper.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Yeah, but that was only in the UK.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:24.000
In the USA, programmes, or rather playbills, continued to be published in the same format.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
While here in the UK, programmes became merely a single sheet of paper

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.000
folded to create four pages for text.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.000
What I don't really get is that after the war, they didn't go back to being more than one sheet

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
or change in any way for over 25 years.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
I know there were paper shortages after the war, but only for 5 or 10 years.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Hmm, strange.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.000
