00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.000
I've found a couple too, Maya. Let's go through and see what we think.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Um, ah, this is an old one for a play called Rui Blas.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Hmm, never heard of that. But the programme looks very decorative.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.000
Good enough to put in a frame on the wall. The images are just beautiful.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Finn, what did you find?

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.000
I've got some pages from a programme for Man of La Mancha.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.000
I thought this was a good programme to show, not because of the pictures,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:06.000
but because it contains articles written by members of the theatre company,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.000
so we can learn how the production was created and the thoughts and feelings of the cast.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Good. I've got a copy of a programme that's now in a museum.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:23.000
It's for The Tragedy of Jane Shore, and it's said to be the earliest surviving document

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.000
to have been printed on Australia's first printing press.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.000
Fantastic!

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Another programme to talk about is for The Sailors' Festival.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.000
It comes from the British Library's digitised collection of programmes that was started a few years ago.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:44.000
It already comprises over 200,000 programmes, which is amazing.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.000
Huh, wish I'd known about it while I was doing my research.

